WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.070
Every art form has one work

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.880
that is so monumental and profound

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.660
that it forever defines
and changes the medium.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.170
For comics, a medium with a
surprisingly long history,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.220
that work is none other than "Watchmen."

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.410
First released in 1986 as
a 12-issue limited series,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:26.500
it forever changed the public
perception of the medium

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.370
and what comics can really achieve.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:30.610
But what makes "Watchmen"

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:32.630
the greatest comic book ever written

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.820
isn't what's on the page,

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:36.743
but the people behind it.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.920
From its inception,
"Watchmen" had one objective:

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.720
to create a work that can only be achieved

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.670
by the comic medium.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.670
And no one played a more integral part

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.060
than the artist Dave Gibbons,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.210
who was given complete
control over its visual look

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:01.110
and focused on making a unique
and distinctive aesthetic

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.850
unlike anything the readers had seen.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.010
Even in the weight of lines,

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.620
where he used a hard, stiff
pen to create bolded edges

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.040
that were significantly different

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.223
from the more fluid lines in
other comic books of the era.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.730
And colorist John Higgins took
the same approach with color,

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.410
choosing to use secondary colors

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.860
that were more common in European comics

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.160
to achieve a moodier look

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.080
and accentuate the use of primary colors,

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.570
like the massive, overflowing
blood from a massacre.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.690
At times, Higgins even freely
experimented with color,

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.320
like in issue No. 6,
"The Abyss Gazes Also,"

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.250
which begins with a brighter palette

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:43.960
that eventually turns darker and darker

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.590
along with the character's
descent into his dark past,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.803
finally ending in black.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:54.130
The genius of "Watchmen"
also lies in its structure,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.040
more specifically its nine-panel grid,

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.820
a simple yet brilliant
way of framing a story

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.990
for multiple reasons.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.381
First, it allowed the writers

00:02:03.381 --> 00:02:05.480
to fit more scenes into a page,

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.940
allowing for a faster-paced story.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.390
It also made it clear
for readers to follow

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.760
because they simply had
to read from left to right

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.890
and top to bottom without
trying to figure out the order.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:19.922
Having nine panels also gave the advantage

00:02:19.922 --> 00:02:24.105
of having a central panel
that naturally draws the eye,

00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:26.730
which both Gibbons and
Higgins expertly used

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.653
to include some of the most
important and shocking images.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.993
And the nine-panel grid
is shockingly versatile,

00:02:34.993 --> 00:02:37.850
with more than 300 different
possible combinations

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.950
the panels can be laid out on,

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.440
a lot of which the book uses
to show a passage of time

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.073
or a gruesome aftermath of a disaster.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.170
The issue that exemplifies

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.400
the genius of the nine-panel structure

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.250
and perhaps the series' greatest

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.560
is its fifth issue, "Fearful Symmetry,"

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.490
where the entire issue, like its title,

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.440
is juxtaposed in a perfect symmetry

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.260
that begins here, in the centerfold image

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.663
in the middle of the book.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.240
The rest of the pages reflect
one another in layout,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.497
color,

00:03:12.497 --> 00:03:13.890
composition,

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.240
imagery,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.768
and sometimes the exact image.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.210
If Gibbons and Higgins showed
the possibility of comics

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.050
as a respectable art form,

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.340
it was "Watchmen" writer Alan Moore

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.250
who elevated them to literature.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:30.960
The genius mind behind some of the most

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.950
influential works in comics,

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.900
like "V for Vendetta"
and "The Killing Joke,"

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.460
Moore played a major role
in creating a comic book

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.283
that feels and reads like literature.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.500
"Watchmen" began with
a simple question of:

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.010
What would superheroes be like
in a credible, real world?

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:51.990
He took the superheroes

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.060
inspired by the campy
heroes of Charlton Comics

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.995
that DC had acquired

00:03:56.995 --> 00:03:58.980
and wrote them into an alternate history

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:01.520
where America won the Vietnam War

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.530
and the Watergate scandal never happened.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.750
This political backdrop created a world

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.090
dripping with end-of-the-world anxieties

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.565
and paranoia of the Cold War

00:04:11.565 --> 00:04:13.240
and provided a grittier look

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.420
at the superheroes who live in it

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:19.330
and that would influence the
genre for decades to come.

00:04:19.330 --> 00:04:21.520
The same goes for the characters.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.080
Originally titled "Who
Killed the Peacemaker?"

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.070
from the Charlton Comics character

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.110
that inspired The Comedian,

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.570
"Watchmen" is constructed
as a murder mystery.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.820
Yet the plot is merely a vehicle

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.350
to show the personal and moral struggles

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:40.160
of the flawed superheroes
living in the real world.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.520
And to accentuate this conflict,

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.360
Moore intentionally wrote characters

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.650
with radically opposing
views of the world.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.770
From a morally absolute vigilante

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:50.710
who sees the world in black and white

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.180
to an omnipotent yet apathetic God figure

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.030
who questions what it means to be human,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.140
"Watchmen" possessed a political
and philosophical depth

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:03.183
that readers weren't familiar
with in comic-book form.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.810
Moore created the idea of "Watchmen,"

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.500
and Gibbons and Higgins built
the world to contain it.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.890
And it's their collaboration

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.648
that ultimately made "Watchmen"

00:05:13.648 --> 00:05:15.840
the masterpiece it is today,

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.020
shown most obviously

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.630
by the book's use of symbols and imagery.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:23.010
Moore wanted recurring symbols and images

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.320
loaded with meaning,

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.250
and Gibbons paid extra attention

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.510
in designing and inserting
them throughout the book.

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:32.880
Some are obvious, like the
bloodstained smiley face,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.400
which bookends the story and
has become an iconic symbol

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:38.510
that represents the series.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.110
The smiley face was designed
to both geometrically

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:44.080
and symbolically represent
a doomsday clock,

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.680
which acts as an
important thematic device.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.510
And others are more subtle,

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.190
like the phrase, "Who
watches the watchmen?" -

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.590
a quote that encompasses
the theme of "Watchmen"

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:57.890
and is graffitied onto the
walls of various panels,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.150
never appearing in its entirety,

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.283
yet still discernible to
those who pay close attention.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:08.020
In fact, "Watchmen" is full
of minute background details

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.990
painstakingly drawn by Gibbons

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.970
that add depth and philosophy to its world

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.660
that are easy to miss on first reading,

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.640
an intentional editorial
choice resulting in a book

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.053
that requires multiple
readings to fully appreciate.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.560
It's these small details and nuances

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.348
that make it so hard to adapt
"Watchmen" for the screen.

00:06:29.348 --> 00:06:31.215
Once deemed "unfilmable,"

00:06:31.215 --> 00:06:33.957
"Watchmen" eventually received
the silver-screen treatment

00:06:33.957 --> 00:06:36.123
by Zack Snyder in 2009.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.400
It's an adaptation that
follows the original

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.150
beat by beat, panel by panel,

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:46.470
resulting in a work that
distinctively looks like "Watchmen"

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.393
but doesn't feel like it.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.715
"Watchmen" is a work that
was envisioned, written,

00:06:52.715 --> 00:06:56.990
and drawn to be a comic
book and nothing else,

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:59.700
the work of two uncompromising artists

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.300
who wanted to test the limits
of the medium that they loved.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.266
And it's for this reason that,

00:07:05.266 --> 00:07:08.622
although we will see many
great works in the future,

00:07:08.622 --> 00:07:10.205
there won't be another work

00:07:10.205 --> 00:07:13.021
quite like "Watchmen" ever again.

